little bit of  buttermilk fold that in  it's going to kind of start to resemble  wet sand so i'm just going to get that  nice and incorporated  and as you can see my breading has  formed an almost shaggy like consistency  you've got these little nice pebbly bits  that are going to flake up on the  outside that means it's time to bring in  my chicken and coat everything so i'm  going to take my chicken directly from  the bag place it into my flour get it  nice and coated and then just flip and  i'm going to press to kind of get those  flaky bits in there  you can see where all the little  breading bits have kind of coated it i'm  just going to place on a wire rack  i'm going to do the same thing with the  rest of my chicken the last thing we  have to do with it is fry it i have a  pot of oil here at about 350 degrees  fahrenheit and i'm going to fry my  chicken in batches two pieces at a time  and lower it in  and  lower it in  so i'm just gonna let this go for about  five to seven minutes checking on it  make sure the color is nice you want the  internal temperature to reach 165 that's  how you know it's done  there's something extremely magical  about watching things fry  alrighty so my chicken looks perfect at  this point so i'm just gonna pull it out  nice and golden brown and i'll place on  a paper towel lined sheet  and then i'm just gonna repeat with my  remaining two pieces  and when you're frying chicken it's  always important to make sure you lay it  away from you in the oil that way none  of it splashes up and burns you all of  my chicken is fried at this point so i'm  just going to remove it from the oil  and then hitting it with a little bit of  salt so i'm just going to toss my  chicken in the oven right now it's set  at about 250 degrees it's not to cook it  just to keep it warm and i'm gonna get  started on my sourdough waffles so in a  large bowl i'm going to combine  all-purpose flour sugar baking powder  and salt and then i'm just going to  whisk until incorporated so now i'm on  my wet ingredients and this is where my  sourdough discard is going to come in  i'm going to add two eggs  of course i'm going to add buttermilk  now i'm going to add my sourdough  discard  now unlike sourdough bread where your  starter has a lot of the yeast that  contributes to the rise in the bread  this is just going to add flavor it's  not going to help my waffles rise all  that much and then finally i'm going to  add my butter and i'm just going to  whisk this until it's nice and combined  and that looks nice and incorporated to  me so now it's time to put everything  together so my dry ingredients are back  i'm just going to add the wet to it and  i'm going to lift this until nice and  combined and i like to go down the sides  just to make sure everything is nice and  incorporated and my batter is basically  done at this point so i'm going to get  my waffle iron and make some waffles it  is the moment of truth my waffle maker  is preheated all i need to do is add my  batter kind of open her up now these  only need about a half cup of batter in  them another one because i don't have a  half cup measuring cup right now and i'm  just going to close it right up and  you're going to let this cook for about  four to six minutes until it's the exact  amount of doneness that you want  looks like it's cooking all right so  it's been about four minutes at this  point i just want to take a little peek  it's looking done enough for me i got  chalked this one up to the oh  wow  i'm just going to repeat this with the  remaining waffles  [Music]  and there you go that is  the last of our waffles all right  so we have fried chicken pretty standard  fried chicken using chicken thighs and  then we have a waffle here but i use the  discard from a sourdough starter so it  should have just like a little bit of  that kind of tartness that comes with  sourdough oh my god it looks incredible  ready yeah  it was good this is why you didn't want  me to